														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2530912">
									

<p>Good that it has been cleaned up. However, I'm not sure if your new series of notes are a true improvement. The talk on &quot;quarternium&quot; and &quot;halved hafnium&quot; feels like techno-babble, especially since the latter is a metal and the SCP is in itself paper. Like, how can hafnium be halved? In what sense is it halved? Yes, even though SCPs defy logic by convention, I think I've reached my limit for this article.</p>
<p>And given that &quot;quarternium&quot; does not exist in Nature, how would the Foundation know it <em>has</em> to be an element?</p>
<p>Overall, the note puts a rather tried story of someone over-estimating themselves in the scientific field.</p>
<p>And I got to question the creator's logic that an infinite toilet paper roll could be his ticket to wealth. If such a thing exists and distributed en masse, the demand to buy toilet papers is mostly gone since the supply is endless. Of course, there is always the question of affordability for these revolutionary devices. But would one use such a thing, as compared to mundane toilet paper?</p>

							</div>

								
